# BonviCall, in front of everything (docs/SECURITY-BACKLOG.md item 1).
#
# Until this file existed the whole system spoke cleartext: a device token, the
# admin password as it is typed, and the audio of every call were readable by
# anyone on the same Wi-Fi. Every other control in the product — Argon2id,
# refresh rotation with reuse detection, tokens stored as SHA-256 — is bypassed
# by reading the wire, so this is the one that had to come first.
#
# The certificate is obtained and renewed by Caddy itself, from Let's Encrypt,
# the first time the domain resolves here. `caddy_data` in the compose file is
# what holds it; losing that volume means asking for a new certificate, and
# Let's Encrypt rate-limits how often that may happen. Back it up or keep it.
{
	email {$BONVICALL_ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$BONVICALL_DOMAIN} {
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	# HSTS. Not in the base header block below, because a wrong max-age here is
	# remembered by every browser that saw it: one year, and only once the
	# domain is genuinely permanent. `includeSubDomains` is left off — this is
	# one host, and claiming the subdomains of a company domain is not ours to
	# do.
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"

	# The three surfaces of the API, the install landing page, and the two
	# health probes. Everything the phone and the panel talk to is here.
	#
	# NO `encode` on this route, and that is load-bearing: audio is served with
	# Range requests so the player can seek, and a compressing proxy answers a
	# range with a body it cannot describe — the browser then withholds
	# `Content-Range` and seeking breaks (CONVENTIONS-CLIENT.md §4). Opus and
	# AAC are already compressed, so there is nothing to win by trying.
	@backend path /api/* /i/* /healthz /readyz
	handle @backend {
		# X-Forwarded-Proto and -Host are set by Caddy itself and are not
		# restated here: `install._public_origin` builds the deep link a
		# handset is pointed at from them, and a `header_up` that repeats a
		# default is a line somebody later "fixes" in the wrong direction.
		#
		# X-Forwarded-For is the exception, and it is OVERWRITTEN rather than
		# appended to. Caddy's default is to append, which leaves whatever the
		# caller sent sitting in front of the real address — and which of the
		# two uvicorn then reports as `request.client.host` is uvicorn's
		# internal choice. Today it takes the rightmost and the real address
		# wins (verified against this stack on 2026-09-14: a request with a
		# forged `X-Forwarded-For` was still counted against its true source).
		# If a future version took the leftmost instead, every per-IP limit in
		# the product would become opt-out by sending one header, silently.
		# Overwriting makes the answer the same either way.
		#
		# It also means Caddy must be the EDGE. Put a CDN in front of this and
		# `{remote_host}` becomes the CDN, so the whole fleet lands in one rate
		# limit bucket — at which point this line changes to trust that hop.
		#
		# The app holds a WebSocket open at /api/device/v1/ws through its
		# foreground service; Caddy upgrades it without being told to.
		reverse_proxy backend:8000 {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		}
	}

	# The panel: a built SPA, copied into this image at build time. Same origin
	# as the API above, which is not a convenience — the audio player requires
	# it, for the Range reason stated above.
	handle {
		root * /srv/panel
		encode zstd gzip
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server

		# Fingerprinted assets are immutable; index.html must never be, or a
		# deployed fix reaches nobody until they clear their cache.
		@assets path /assets/*
		header @assets Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
		@index path /
		header @index Cache-Control "no-cache"
	}

	# The APK upload is 200 MiB (SPEC §4.0) and an audio chunk is 4 MiB. This
	# sits above both on purpose: the server's own 413 — in the error envelope
	# the Android client can parse — is a better answer than a refusal from a
	# proxy, which the client would see as a corrupted body and could not
	# decide whether to retry.
	request_body {
		max_size 256MB
	}
}
